External binding packages expose typed functions, constants and opaque types implemented outside Moth. The HTML builder currently supports annotated project-local JavaScript files; WIT component imports are deferred.

### Annotated JavaScript dependencies

The HTML builder supports annotated single-file `.js` dependency clauses through `@moth.opaque`, `@moth.sig` and `@moth.const`. `@moth.sig` declares each exposed function and `@moth.const` declares each literal-backed constant. `@moth.opaque` is required only when signatures use foreign opaque handle types. A function-only module may use `@moth.sig` without `@moth.opaque` and a constant-only module may use `@moth.const` on its own. JavaScript export names are runtime implementation details. Moth names come from annotations.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @vendor/drawing.js as drawing
]

#### Annotated JavaScript example

[codeblock, $code("js"):
    /**
     * @moth.sig emphasize |text String| -> String
     */
    export const emphasize = (text) => {
        return `**${text}**`;
    };


    /**
     * @moth.sig join_label |prefix String, body String| -> String
     */
    export function joinLabel(prefix, body) {
        return `${prefix}: ${body}`;
    }
]

The Moth dependency clause uses the annotation names, not the JavaScript export names:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @vendor/drawing.js emphasize, join_label
]

#### Supported JavaScript export forms

- `export function name(...) { ... }`
- block-bodied arrow exports
- one `export const NAME = 4;` declarator with an unsigned decimal literal for `@moth.const`

`@moth.sig` annotations expose free functions and `@moth.const` annotations expose literal constants. `this` receiver-style signatures are rejected during registration. Runtime imports from builder-registered modules must be named static imports.

#### Exact signature subset

- parameters are positional and typed
- mutable parameters use `name ~Type`
- signatures have zero or one success return
- an optional final `Error!` channel is allowed
- `Error!` must be final
- numeric slots spell `I32`, `I64`, `U32`, `U64`, `F32` or `F64`, alongside `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and opaque handle types
- the exact numeric cutover does not add `I8`, `I16`, `U8`, `U16`, `F16`, `Byte` or Dec annotation support
- generic signatures are rejected
- option and collection types are rejected
- callbacks are rejected
- multi-success returns are rejected
- `this` receiver-shaped signatures are rejected for external package registration
- opaque types must be declared through `@moth.opaque` before use where required
- JavaScript export names are runtime implementation details
- Moth names come from annotations

#### Exact literal constant subset

`@moth.const` exposes a flat Moth name and the fixed `U32` type. The JavaScript export name may differ:

[codeblock, $code("js"):
    /** @moth.const primitive_triangles U32 */
    export const TRIANGLES = 4;
]

The initializer supplies the value. Compilation reads its authored literal without executing JavaScript and preserves its exact `U32` identity through imports and flat direct-selection source re-exports, independently of the native numeric profile.

Each annotation accepts one `export const` declarator with an unsigned decimal integer literal from `0` through `4294967295`. Whitespace and comments may separate tokens. A semicolon may close the declaration, or a newline may end it when the following token doesn't continue the initializer.

Unannotated constants, leading-zero spellings other than `0`, numeric separators, alternate radices, signs, fractions, exponents, BigInt suffixes, arithmetic, references, property reads, calls, multiple declarators and non-`U32` constant annotations remain rejected. `@moth.sig` cannot annotate a literal constant and `@moth.const` cannot annotate a callable export. Duplicate Moth names across functions, constants and opaque types, and duplicate JavaScript export names, also produce diagnostics.

Constant-only dependencies contribute semantic inputs without emitting their JavaScript asset, wrappers or authored runtime imports. A literal change still invalidates its semantic users even when no asset is emitted, so a `4` to `5` edit rebuilds what depends on it. A dependency with reachable functions retains the normal runtime asset once. Nested annotation names and nominal foreign enum domains remain pending.

#### Rejected JavaScript features

- arbitrary dependency graphs
- default exports
- re-exports
- CommonJS
- classes
- constants outside the exact `@moth.const` literal subset
- property accessors
- callbacks
- async functions
- collections or options in JS signatures
- generic external type
- receiver methods
- multi-success JS returns

### Restricted host-binding profile

Existing Core, Builder and JavaScript-backed bindings follow a restricted host-binding profile:

- ordinary Moth values cross by value
- host code may not retain references into ordinary Moth storage
- opaque handles represent foreign identities rather than Moth reference types

Observable external resources require explicit close or teardown. Host finalisation timing does not define language behaviour.

### Compiler-generated runtime error exports

`@moth/runtime` exposes `mothOk` and `mothErr` unchanged alongside five named numeric error exports. The names are `MOTH_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED`, `MOTH_ERROR_HOST_INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `MOTH_ERROR_HOST_RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND`, `MOTH_ERROR_HOST_RESOURCE_UNAVAILABLE` and `MOTH_ERROR_HOST_OPERATION_FAILED`. `BuiltinErrorCode` owns their values and one association generates both the validated exported-name list and the numeric JavaScript declarations. Package assets import them by name and never copy numbers into a second table or static asset.

### Numeric signature types

External numeric signatures use exact Moth types separately from their foreign ABI carriers. Ordinary default-language registrations migrate to I32, U32 and F64. Already fixed foreign signatures retain their widths. Annotated JavaScript's numeric subset is I32, I64, U32, U64, F32 and F64, not every language numeric type. This accepted cutover remains pending in the Alpha compiler, whose current native signatures still use numeric profiles. See @../progress (Progress).

- I64 crosses annotated JavaScript as a `Number` only within the safe-integer range, from -9,007,199,254,740,991 through 9,007,199,254,740,991 inclusive. Values outside that range fail rather than silently losing precision.
- U64 inside Moth JavaScript is a `BigInt` from `0` through 18446744073709551615. Across the existing `Number`-based foreign adapter it crosses only from `0` through 9007199254740991. That restriction belongs to the foreign boundary, not the Moth U64 range.
- The U64 adapter rejects negative or unsafe values before precision can be lost. It validates one evaluated snapshot, canonicalises `Number` `-0` to integer `0` and rejects fractions, non-finite values and wrong carrier types. Foreign contract violations throw rather than creating a new implicit-failure or `Error` code.
- U32 crosses annotated JavaScript as a `Number` integer from `0` through 4294967295. The wrapper does not coerce or wrap and rejects invalid carriers or ranges. Integer `-0` canonicalises to `0` at ingress. Infallible and fallible calls validate successful payloads identically, evaluating each foreign result once.
- An F32 parameter must already be an exact finite binary32 `Number`. The wrapper checks `typeof`, finiteness and `Math.fround` equality before the foreign call observes the value. It preserves signed zero and subnormals. Inexact, non-finite or wrong-carrier values throw.
- An F32 result rounds once at binary32 with `Math.fround` and must stay finite. The wrapper evaluates the foreign value once and preserves signed zero and subnormals. A non-`Number` fails at the same boundary. A rounded non-finite value throws in an infallible wrapper and becomes builtin `Error` code `304` through the documented builtin `Error!` boundary.
- F64 results retain binary64 width and undergo the shared finite-result check. A non-finite result uses code `304` through a declared builtin `Error!` boundary, otherwise the integrity guard halts.
- I32 values must be JavaScript Numbers, integral and within -2,147,483,648 through 2,147,483,647. Invalid successful payloads fail at the wrapper boundary in both infallible and fallible calls. Integer ingress converts `-0` to `0`, while binary-float ingress preserves signed zero.
- Opaque external types retain their package-specific identities and representations; numeric carrier adaptation does not reinterpret them.

Moth-owned finite-value checks, including the shared external binary-float
boundary with code `304`, are fatal integrity guards, not implicit built-in failure:
they halt unless a declared builtin `Error!` contract returns them. This changes
failure delivery, not numeric validity or the foreign signature.
See @../errors (Errors) and @../progress (Progress) for the contract and current
support.

Validation uses the resolved exact result type and precision for every
supported result shape, not a package-name test. Unsupported shapes retain
their capability rejection. Validate only successful payloads and preserve a
valid returned Error's code, message and identity. Authored safety catch never
creates an Error producer or makes a fatal integrity guard recoverable.

Fallible JavaScript bindings project `error.code` onto Moth's `U32` field,
without numeric-profile dependence. The wrapper reads the foreign code once and
accepts JavaScript Number integers from `0` through `4294967295`. It normalises
integer `-0` to `0` and uses code `0` for missing or invalid codes.
The existing adapter for declared fallible JavaScript bindings turns exceptions
thrown by the export into builtin `Error` values with code `0`. Their message
uses a truthy `message` property or stringifies the thrown value, including
`null` and `undefined`. Current invalid-wrapper handling remains
build-mode-specific; see @../progress (Progress). Implicit numeric failure adds
no automatic recoverable adapter for unexpected foreign throws or other
violations of a declared value contract.

### Deferred WIT component support

WIT is the standard foreign Wasm/component boundary. It does not encode Moth module or package semantic interfaces. `MothSource` packages keep their Moth interfaces even when their physical output is Wasm.

The HTML builder does not currently load WIT component imports. Component discovery and projection into binding-backed package metadata are deferred; no WIT component loader contract is delivered here.

### Deferred WIT surfaces

No WIT component contract is delivered for resources, callbacks, async operations, futures, streams, shared-memory views, raw pointers, returned aliases or retained Moth references.

### Concepts related to external binding contracts

- For package origin and backing see @#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing).
- For dependency path rules see @#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
- For Core package API details see @./core (Core packages).
- For Builder package API details see @./builder (Builder packages).
